fihrist:manuscript_26538 a mdhn:Manuscript ;
    rdfs:label "Persian MS 666" ;
    dct:date "1851-09-29" ;
    dct:description """Author Mansā Rām Munshī
                                Khūshābi first composed this ornate Persian prose
                            redaction of the Punjabi tragic romance by Damodar Das Arora (1486-1568) of
                            legendary lovers Hīr and Rajnhā in 1157 AH (1744 CE) . An
                            unnamed son of Mīyan ‘Umar ibn Ḥafiẓ Muḥammad , from
                            a family of dyers in Jhang Sī'āl , completed this manuscript for Lachhmī Narāyan S'hāī Jīv on 5 Ẕī-al-Ḥajj [al-Ḥijjah]
                                1267 AH (29 September 1851 CE) , who probably presented it as
                            a gift to George William Hamilton
                                (1807–1868) , who then served as Deputy Commissioner
                            there. The story of the lovers ends with their death in the town, where
                            their mausoleum still attracts love-striken couples today.""" ;
    dct:identifier "manuscript_26538" ;
    dct:language "fa" ;
    dct:type "codex" ;
    mdhn:digitallyPublishedBy glam:publisher_The_University_of_Manchester_Library ;
    mdhn:hasContribution contrib:manuscript_26538_person_22161693743415930845_cataloguer,
