fihrist:manuscript_26591 a mdhn:Manuscript ;
    rdfs:label "Persian MS 991" ;
    dct:date "1740-10-08" ;
    dct:description """A copy of the Bahār-i Dānish (Spring of Wisdom) by ʻInāyat
                        Allāh (d. ca. 1671) , relates the romantic adventures of Jahāndār
                     Sulṭān and Bahravar Bānū, which serves as a frame story interwoven with other
                     tales. An unidentified scribe completed this volume in Awadh on 17 Rajab 1153 AH (8 Oct. 1740
                        CE) , possibly in Awadh, where the French mercenary Jean-Baptiste Joseph Gentil (d. 1799) . One of six
                     copies held in the Rylands, this redaction lacks the preface by the author's
                     younger brother and pupil Muḥammad
                        Ṣāliḥ often found in other volumes, and instead opens with his
                     own introduction, in which he explains how he adapted the tale from one that he
                     heard a young Brahmin recite. The volume also contains a glossary appended to
                     the text that explains many terms.""" ;
    dct:identifier "manuscript_26591" ;
    dct:language "fa" ;
    dct:spatial "Awadh" ;
    dct:type "codex" ;
    mdhn:digitallyPublishedBy glam:publisher_The_University_of_Manchester_Library ;
